      "Clear service and parts channel through the installer"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Not available at retail, so pricing is through the contractor",
      "Fewer smart features than some competitors"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "A solid choice for homeowners who want a plumber-supplied unit with a clear support chain.",
    "description": "Bradford White sells only through professional plumbers, and that distribution shapes what the AeroTherm series is: a heat pump water heater designed with the installer in mind and supported through the contractor who puts it in. The series covers tank sizes from 50 to 80 gallons, so your plumber can match capacity to the household without switching brands, and it offers several operating modes from efficiency-focused to high demand. Bradford White has a long-standing reputation among plumbers for durable tanks, and the AeroTherm line extends that to hybrid units. It is less focused on apps and connected features than Rheem, which some owners see as a plus and others as a gap. Because you buy through a contractor, pricing includes their markup and labor, which makes direct comparison harder but gives you a single point of contact for service and warranty claims. If you already have a plumber you trust who carries Bradford White, this is a straightforward, well-supported option that will be installed correctly the first time."
  },
  {
    "name": "Ruud Ultra Series Hybrid 50 Gallon",
    "badge": "Best Value",
    "category": "240V Hybrid, 50 Gallon",
    "amazonUrl": "",
    "id": "ruud-ultra-hybrid",
    "pros": [
      "Shares much of its engineering with Rheem hybrids",
      "High efficiency rating for the price",
      "Multiple operating modes",
      "Often priced below the ProTerra",
      "Sold through contractors, with broad parts availability"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Fewer retail channels, so typically bought through an installer",
      "Connected features vary by version"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "Much of the ProTerra's capability at a lower price, if your installer carries Ruud.",
    "description": "Ruud and Rheem are sister brands under the same parent company, and Ruud's Ultra Series hybrid shares much of its engineering with Rheem's heat pump water heaters. Its efficiency rating sits in the same high range, it offers comparable operating modes, and parts and service knowledge overlap heavily, which matters for long-term support. The difference is mainly in distribution. Ruud is sold primarily through contractors rather than home centers, so you will usually buy it as part of an installation quote. That can work in your favor; contractors who carry Ruud often price the Ultra hybrid below a ProTerra for comparable capacity. Connected features vary by version, so if leak alerts or app control matter to you, confirm the specific model before signing. Owners and installers tend to describe it as a dependable, efficient unit without much to distinguish it from its Rheem sibling other than the badge and the price. If you are collecting quotes and one comes back with a Ruud hybrid at a lower price, there is little reason to hold out for the Rheem."
  },
  {
    "name": "SanCO2 Split Heat Pump Water Heater",
    "badge": "Best for Cold Climates",
    "category": "CO2 Split System, Outdoor Compressor",
    "amazonUrl": "",
    "id": "sanden-sanco2-split",
    "pros": [
      "Outdoor heat pump uses CO2 refrigerant and is rated to operate well below freezing per the maker",
      "Indoor tank has no heat pump on top, so no indoor noise or cooling",
      "Very high delivered water temperatures",
      "Long expected service life",
      "Works where indoor air space is limited"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Expensive, often several times the cost of an integrated hybrid",
      "Requires a specialist installer and outdoor unit placement"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "A premium solution for cold climates or tight homes where an integrated hybrid cannot draw enough warm air.",
    "description": "The SanCO2 takes the heat pump out of the house. A compact outdoor unit using carbon dioxide as its refrigerant heats water and sends it to a separate indoor storage tank through insulated lines. That design solves two problems integrated hybrids struggle with. First, the indoor tank does not cool or dehumidify the room, and it makes no compressor noise inside. Second, the CO2 heat pump is rated by the maker to keep working at outdoor temperatures well below freezing, while integrated hybrids rely on indoor air staying above roughly the 40s. CO2 systems also produce very hot water efficiently, which lets a smaller tank deliver more usable hot water through a mixing valve. The trade-offs are cost and installation. Expect the system to cost several times more than an integrated hybrid at the time of writing, and you will need an installer familiar with split systems, plus an outdoor location for the unit. For a tight, well-insulated home in a cold region, or a house with no suitable indoor space, it is the strongest heat pump option available."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Heat Pump Water Heater",
          item: "https://www.allmetalbrake.com/best-heat-pump-water-heater/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Which heat pump water heater should most households buy?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For most homes with a basement or garage and a 240-volt circuit, the Rheem ProTerra 50-gallon hybrid offers the best mix of efficiency, controls, and warranty. Larger households should step up to a 65 or 66-gallon model such as the A.O. Smith Voltex AL. If adding a 240-volt circuit is costly, a 120-volt plug-in model sized generously is the practical alternative."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is it worth replacing a working gas water heater with a heat pump?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It depends on local gas and electricity prices, available rebates, and whether new electrical work is needed. Payback against a gas tank is often longer than against an electric resistance tank and may not arrive where gas is cheap. Many homeowners wait until the gas heater nears the end of its life, then compare installed costs and running costs side by side."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How cold can the space be?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Most integrated units keep using the heat pump down to somewhere around 37 to 45 degrees, depending on the model, and switch to resistance heat below that. Efficiency also drops as temperatures fall. Spaces that stay above the high 40s give the best results. For cold garages or climates, a split system with an outdoor CO2 heat pump is a better fit."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How loud is a heat pump water heater?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Typical published sound levels sit roughly in the mid-40s to mid-50s decibels, similar to a refrigerator or a quiet window air conditioner. In a basement or garage, most owners stop noticing it. Avoid installing one in a closet next to a bedroom or main living area if noise would bother you."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Does a heat pump water heater dehumidify the room?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Yes. As it pulls heat from the air, it also removes moisture, which drains away as condensate. In a damp basement, that can noticeably lower humidity in summer and reduce the load on a dehumidifier. It is not a substitute for proper moisture control if the basement has water problems, but it helps."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What maintenance does a heat pump water heater need?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Clean the air filter every few months, check that the condensate drain flows freely, drain a few gallons from the tank annually to reduce sediment, test the relief valve each year, and check the anode rod every few years where the design includes one. Connected models often send filter and fault alerts through their app."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Do I need a new circuit for a heat pump water heater?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "A 240-volt hybrid needs a dedicated 240-volt circuit, typically 30 amps, which is usually already present when replacing an electric tank. Replacing a gas tank may require running a new circuit, and possibly a panel upgrade. 120-volt plug-in models avoid that, and some can share an existing circuit, at the cost of slower recovery."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(Astro.url.pathname),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "All Metal Brake" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: authorName(Astro.url.pathname),
    section: "Air, Mold & Water Heaters",
    tags: [
      "best heat pump water heater",
      "hybrid water heater",
      "120v heat pump water heater",
      "heat pump water heater cost",
      "water heater efficiency",
      "uef rating"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10" style="background-image: radial-gradient(circle at 20% 20%, #38BDF8 0, transparent 45%), radial-gradient(circle at 80% 60%, #FF4500 0, transparent 40%);"></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
