        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="9.3k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. Top Picks</a>
          <a href="#how-it-saves" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. How They Save</a>
          <a href="#types" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. 240V, 120V, or Split</a>
          <a href="#sizing" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Sizing Up</a>
          <a href="#space" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Where It Can Go</a>
          <a href="#cold" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Cold Spaces</a>
          <a href="#payback" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Running Cost & Payback</a>
          <a href="#install" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Install & Incentives</a>
          <a href="#noise" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Noise & Condensate</a>
          <a href="#modes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Modes & Settings</a>
          <a href="#upkeep" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">11. Upkeep</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">12. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">13. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Electric resistance water heaters turn one unit of electricity into roughly one unit of heat. A heat pump model pulls warmth out of the room air and pumps it into the water instead, and the best models deliver three to four units of hot water energy for each unit of electricity. That is the whole case for them. The catch is that they need room to breathe, they work best in spaces that stay above the 40s, and they recover more slowly than the tank you are used to.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />


        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The 6 Best Heat Pump Water Heaters, Ranked
          </h2>

          <p>
            Picks are ranked from published Uniform Energy Factor and first-hour ratings, operating ranges, air-space requirements, warranty terms, installation needs, and recurring patterns in owner and installer feedback. We did not test these units in a lab or a home; every figure below is attributed to the maker or given as a typical range.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} index={index} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="how-it-saves" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. How a Heat Pump Water Heater Saves Energy
          </h2>

          <p>
            An integrated heat pump water heater looks like a conventional electric tank with a compressor and fan on top. The fan pulls in surrounding air, an evaporator coil absorbs heat from it, the compressor raises that heat to a higher temperature, and a condenser transfers it into the water in the tank. The cooled, drier air leaves the unit. It is the same principle as a refrigerator, run in reverse.
          </p>

          <p>
            The efficiency gain comes from moving heat instead of making it. A resistance element converts electricity directly into heat, so its efficiency is close to 100 percent at best. A heat pump uses electricity to move heat that already exists in the air, so it can deliver several times more heat energy than it consumes. That is why the best units carry Uniform Energy Factors between roughly 3.5 and 4, compared with about 0.9 for a resistance tank.
          </p>

          <p>
            Most residential models are hybrids. They also contain resistance elements, which kick in when demand outpaces the heat pump or when the surrounding air is too cold. Operating modes let you decide how readily those elements engage. Running in heat-pump-only or efficiency mode maximizes savings; hybrid or high-demand modes trade some efficiency for faster recovery.
          </p>

          <p>
            Federal efficiency standards are pushing the market toward heat pumps. The Department of Energy finalized rules in 2024 that, starting in 2029, effectively require heat pump technology for most new electric storage water heaters in common sizes. Buying one now gets you the efficiency early, while installers are already building experience with them.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-4">Heat Pump vs Resistance, Typical Figures</p>
            <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-4 text-center">
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">3.5-4.0</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">UEF, top hybrids</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">About 0.9</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">UEF, resistance tank</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-white">60-75%</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Typical electricity cut</p>
              </div>
              <div>
                <p class="text-3xl font-black text-[#38BDF8]">10-15 yrs</p>
                <p class="text-gray-400 text-xs mt-1">Expected lifespan range</p>
              </div>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="types" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. The Three Types: 240V, 120V, and Split
          </h2>

          <p>
            The 240-volt integrated hybrid is the most common type, and the most flexible. It has a heat pump on top and resistance elements in the tank, needs a dedicated 240-volt circuit, typically 30 amps, and recovers quickly in hybrid mode. If you are replacing an electric resistance tank, the circuit is usually already there, which makes the swap relatively simple.
          </p>

          <p>
            The 120-volt plug-in hybrid is designed for homes where running a new 240-volt circuit is expensive, often because the panel is full or the heater is far from it. It plugs into a standard outlet, and some versions can share a circuit. It relies mostly on the heat pump, so recovery is slower, and sizing the tank larger is the key to living with it. These units are especially useful when replacing a gas water heater.
          </p>

          <p>
            Split systems put the heat pump outdoors and the tank indoors, connected by refrigerant or water lines. They handle cold climates better, make no indoor noise, and do not cool the room. They also cost far more and need specialist installation. For most homes, an integrated hybrid is the practical choice; split systems are for specific situations.
          </p>

