        <div id="sizing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Sizing: Go One Step Larger
          </h2>

          <p>
            The single most important sizing rule for heat pump water heaters is to go bigger than you think. In heat-pump-only mode, recovery is slower than a resistance tank and much slower than gas. A household that was satisfied with a 40-gallon electric tank will often be happier with a 50 or 65, and a household that used a 50 is a good candidate for a 65 or 66.
          </p>

          <p>
            Use the first-hour rating, which is printed on the EnergyGuide label, to compare models. Estimate your busiest hour, usually the morning, by adding up showers at roughly 10 to 20 gallons each, a dishwasher at about 6 gallons, and any laundry on warm. The heater's first-hour rating should comfortably exceed that total in the mode you plan to run.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
