                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">80 gallon, or 65 plus hybrid mode</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            A larger tank also helps efficiency. It gives the heat pump more time to work without calling on the resistance elements, which is where most of the savings are lost. Standby loss rises slightly with tank size, but on a well-insulated modern tank the difference is small compared with the benefit of avoiding resistance heat.
          </p>

          <p>
            Consider the household's direction. If teenagers are on the way, or guests visit often, size for the busier future. A heat pump water heater is a 10 to 15 year purchase, and swapping it early for a larger one costs far more than buying the right size now.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="space" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Where You Can Put One
          </h2>

          <p>
            An integrated heat pump water heater needs a supply of air to draw heat from. Manufacturers generally call for several hundred cubic feet of surrounding space, often in the range of 450 to 1,000 depending on the model, or a smaller space with louvered doors or ducting. A typical basement or garage meets that easily; a small utility closet does not.
          </p>

          <p>
            Height is the next constraint. The heat pump module sits on top of the tank, so these units are taller than conventional tanks, often reaching six feet or more. Measure from the floor to the lowest obstruction, such as ducts, pipes, or joists, and add clearance for servicing the filter and top components as listed in the manual.
          </p>

          <p>
            The unit cools and dehumidifies its surroundings. In a warm basement or garage, that is a welcome side effect; in summer, a heat pump water heater can lower humidity in a damp basement, which reduces how hard a dehumidifier from our <a href="/best-dehumidifier-for-basement/">basement dehumidifier guide</a> has to work. In a space that shares air with the living area in winter, the cooling effect will slightly increase heating load.
          </p>

          <p>
            Avoid locations that are dusty or prone to lint, such as right beside a clothes dryer, because the air filter will clog quickly. Avoid rooms where the noise will bother anyone, such as a closet next to a bedroom. And make sure there is a floor drain, a sink, or a condensate pump nearby, because condensate drips out steadily any time the compressor is running.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cold" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Cold Garages and Unheated Spaces
          </h2>

          <p>
            Integrated heat pump water heaters draw heat from the air around them, so the colder that air, the less heat there is to work with. Efficiency falls as temperatures drop, and below a cutoff, typically somewhere around 37 to 45 degrees depending on the model, the unit switches to its resistance elements entirely. At that point it behaves like an ordinary electric tank.
          </p>

          <p>
            That makes location climate matter. A basement that stays in the 50s and 60s year-round is close to ideal. An attached garage in a mild climate also works well. A detached or uninsulated garage in a cold climate may push the unit into resistance mode for several months each winter, cutting annual savings considerably.
          </p>

          <p>
            There are workarounds. Ducting the intake and exhaust to warmer spaces, or to the outdoors in summer only, can help, though it adds cost and complexity. Insulating and air-sealing the garage raises its winter temperature. Or, in genuinely cold climates with no good indoor location, a split system like the SanCO2, with its outdoor CO2 heat pump, avoids the problem.
          </p>

          <p>
            Before buying, place a min-max thermometer in the proposed location through a cold snap. If the space rarely drops below the high 40s, an integrated hybrid will deliver most of its rated savings. If it spends weeks in the 30s, plan on either a different location, a split system, or lower expected savings.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="payback" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Running Cost and Honest Payback
          </h2>

          <p>
            A typical household using an electric resistance tank spends somewhere in the range of $400 to $700 a year on water heating, depending on usage and electricity price. A heat pump water heater running mostly on the heat pump often cuts that by 60 to 75 percent, saving very roughly $250 to $500 a year. The EnergyGuide label on each model shows an estimated yearly cost at national average prices, which is a useful way to compare specific units.
          </p>

          <p>
            Payback depends on what you are replacing. Against an electric resistance tank, where the electrical circuit already exists, the extra cost of a heat pump model is often recovered within a few years. Against a gas tank, the math is less certain: gas is cheaper per unit of heat in many areas, and the switch may involve new electrical work, so payback can stretch to many years or never arrive if gas prices are low.
          </p>

          <p>
            Incentives can shift the numbers dramatically. Utility rebates of several hundred dollars are common in some areas, and state programs can be larger. Factor them in before deciding, but read the eligibility rules closely, since many require specific models, income limits, or installation by a participating contractor.
          </p>

