        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            13. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which heat pump water heater should most households buy?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                For most homes with a basement or garage and a 240-volt circuit, the Rheem ProTerra 50-gallon hybrid offers the best mix of efficiency, controls, and warranty. Larger households should step up to a 65 or 66-gallon model such as the A.O. Smith Voltex AL. If adding a 240-volt circuit is costly, a 120-volt plug-in model sized generously is the practical alternative.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it worth replacing a working gas water heater with a heat pump?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                It depends on local gas and electricity prices, available rebates, and whether new electrical work is needed. Payback against a gas tank is often longer than against an electric resistance tank and may not arrive where gas is cheap. Many homeowners wait until the gas heater nears the end of its life, then compare installed costs and running costs side by side.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How cold can the space be?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Most integrated units keep using the heat pump down to somewhere around 37 to 45 degrees, depending on the model, and switch to resistance heat below that. Efficiency also drops as temperatures fall. Spaces that stay above the high 40s give the best results. For cold garages or climates, a split system with an outdoor CO2 heat pump is a better fit.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How loud is a heat pump water heater?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Typical published sound levels sit roughly in the mid-40s to mid-50s decibels, similar to a refrigerator or a quiet window air conditioner. In a basement or garage, most owners stop noticing it. Avoid installing one in a closet next to a bedroom or main living area if noise would bother you.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does a heat pump water heater dehumidify the room?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. As it pulls heat from the air, it also removes moisture, which drains away as condensate. In a damp basement, that can noticeably lower humidity in summer and reduce the load on a dehumidifier. It is not a substitute for proper moisture control if the basement has water problems, but it helps.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">What maintenance does a heat pump water heater need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Clean the air filter every few months, check that the condensate drain flows freely, drain a few gallons from the tank annually to reduce sediment, test the relief valve each year, and check the anode rod every few years where the design includes one. Connected models often send filter and fault alerts through their app.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Do I need a new circuit for a heat pump water heater?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A 240-volt hybrid needs a dedicated 240-volt circuit, typically 30 amps, which is usually already present when replacing an electric tank. Replacing a gas tank may require running a new circuit, and possibly a panel upgrade. 120-volt plug-in models avoid that, and some can share an existing circuit, at the cost of slower recovery.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

